# Source: built from HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Single assignments", items 3 and 4 (the two assignments are put in sequence).
id: assign-constant
title: Two assignments to the same variable
topic: Assignment
level: 1
source: Built from HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Single assignments" 3 and 4
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, consequence

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$, statement by statement from the last one. Then use the consequence
  rule to decide whether the triple is valid.

pre: y = 2
post: x = 7 && x > y

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  x := 7;          // i1
  {R1}
  x := y + 1       // i2
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := y + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression ($y + 1$) in the
  postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := y + 1, Q)
  = (x = 7 && x > y)[x <- y + 1]
  = y + 1 = 7 && y + 1 > y     // every x is replaced by y + 1
  = y = 6 && y + 1 > y         // subtract 1 from both sides of y + 1 = 7
  = y = 6 && true              // y + 1 > y holds for every integer y
  = y = 6                      // A && true is A
set: R1 = y = 6

step: R2, the weakest precondition of x := 7
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression
  ($7$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$.
calc:
  wp(x := 7, R1)
  = (y = 6)[x <- 7]
  = y = 6           // x does not occur in y = 6, so nothing changes
set: R2 = y = 6

step: P implies R2
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R2
text:
  $R2$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R2$.

  >>> «P» ==> «R2»
vc: y = 2 ==> y = 6
status: fails
counterexample: y = 2
why:
  The state $y = 2$ satisfies $P$ and does not satisfy $y = 6$. From this
  state the program ends with $x = 3$, so $x = 7$ is false at the end.
result: The triple is not valid.

summary:
  The value $7$ given to $x$ by the first assignment is overwritten by the
  second one, so it does not appear in the weakest precondition. The
  program establishes $Q$ only when $y = 6$ at the start.
